window.docContents[92].resolve({"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Energy-and-Weak-Boundary-Value-Problems--Weak-Elliptic-Problems--Principal-Divergence-Form-Problems--Principal-forms-and-ellipticity":{"contents":"The principal divergence-form equation replaces the identity matrix in\nPoisson's equation by a coefficient field:\n\n\n\nWe first represent the coefficients as a finite matrix of bounded\noperators on .  Pointwise multiplication by an\n matrix field recovers the local PDE model, while the\noperator-valued form is also meaningful for bounded nonlocal coefficients.\n\nAlmost-everywhere pointwise ellipticity of the matrix field is integrated against the weak gradient.\nThat produces coercivity of the principal form after Poincaré controls the\n coordinate.  Lax–Milgram then constructs the unique element of\n, and the smooth-test bridge identifies its variational\nequation with the displayed divergence-form PDE.\n\nEach hypothesis has one job: measurability and\nessential boundedness define the multiplication operators, ellipticity\ncontrols the gradient, boundedness of the domain supplies Poincaré, and\nopenness supplies compactly supported smooth test functions.\n\nThe principal divergence-form bilinear form contracts the weak gradients of\nthe trial and test functions against a matrix of bounded operators on\n. Pointwise multiplication operators give an \ncoefficient matrix; arbitrary bounded operators give nonlocal coefficients.\n\nFor any matrix of bounded operators the form  is bounded, but nothing so\nfar gives it a lower bound. The Lax–Milgram argument only ever sees the\nquadratic form  on , so the weakest usable\nellipticity hypothesis is stated directly at that level, as a comparison with\nthe Dirichlet form.\n\nAn operator-valued coefficient matrix is integrated-uniformly elliptic with\nconstant  when its principal quadratic form dominates\n times the Dirichlet form on every  field.  This is exactly\nthe energy inequality needed by the abstract coercivity argument.\n\nThe pointwise ellipticity hypothesis constrains the matrix  for almost\nevery  and every direction . It only makes\nsense for multiplication operators, which is why it is a separate definition\nrather than a special case of the integrated one.\n\nFor a measurable  matrix field , Evans's pointwise condition\nrequires a single  such that, for almost every  and every\n, one hasThe matrix need not be symmetric: only its symmetric part contributes to this\nquadratic inequality, and the coercivity result below applies to nonsymmetric\ncoefficients satisfying the same condition.\n\nThe two notions are compatible in the expected direction. Choosing\n in the pointwise inequality and integrating over \nrecovers the integrated inequality for the multiplication realization, with\nthe same constant .\n\nAlmost-everywhere pointwise ellipticity of an  matrix field\nimplies integrated ellipticity of its multiplication-operator realization.\n\nApply the pointwise matrix inequality to the measurable vector of weak\nderivatives and integrate.  Hölder integrability of each coefficient-gradient\nproduct justifies the finite sums and integrals.\n\nThe integrated hypothesis feeds the Hilbert-space theory, while the PDE\nreading of the problem needs the concrete  coefficients and\ncompactly supported smooth tests. As for Poisson's equation, the\ndistributional predicate carries the openness of  with it.\n\nFor an open measurable domain, ,\n, and , the weak-solution predicate\nstores the openness of  and recordsfor every . This is the concrete\ndistributional equation for . A separate density\ntheorem identifies it with the operator-valued variational identity tested\nagainst every .\n\nCoercivity is proved at the operator-valued level from the integrated\nhypothesis alone. The ellipticity constant  only compares  with\nthe Dirichlet form, so the bounded-domain Poincaré inequality is still needed,\nexactly as for the Dirichlet form itself, to control the base \ncoordinate.\n\nLet  be a matrix of bounded operators on . On a\nbounded measurable domain in positive dimension, suppose  is\nintegrated-uniformly elliptic: for some ,Then the variable-coefficient form satisfies\n for some . For a pointwise\n coefficient matrix, the preceding almost-everywhere-to-integrated\ntheorem supplies this hypothesis from Evans's pointwise ellipticity condition.\n\nIntegrated ellipticity bounds the operator-valued principal form below by\n times the Dirichlet form. The bounded-domain Poincaré estimate makes\nthe Dirichlet form coercive for the full graph-space norm, and multiplication\nof the two positive constants gives the result.\n\nLet  be open, measurable, and bounded with . If\n is uniformly elliptic almost everywhere, then every\n admits exactly one  satisfying\n\nRepresent the coefficient field by its bounded multiplication operators,\ndeduce coercivity from almost-everywhere ellipticity, apply Lax–Milgram, and\ntranslate the Hilbert-space equation back to the concrete weak integral\nidentity.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Elliptic Equations\u0009Energy and Weak Boundary-Value Problems\u0009Weak Elliptic Problems\u0009Principal Divergence-Form Problems","header":"5.4.2.4.1. Principal forms and ellipticity","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Energy-and-Weak-Boundary-Value-Problems--Weak-Elliptic-Problems--Principal-Divergence-Form-Problems--Principal-forms-and-ellipticity"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Sobolev-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--The-two-Sobolev-models--Fourier-Bessel-potential-spaces":{"contents":"The Fourier model is a whole-space Hilbert scale\n for every real order .  Its elements are tempered\ndistributions whose Fourier transforms become square integrable after\nmultiplication by\n\n\n\nThis model is the one used for:\n\n* fractional and negative regularity;* a Hilbert space at every real order;* exact scale inclusions and Bessel-potential shifts;* distributional weak derivatives at arbitrary real order;* the comparison with integer-order .\n\nThe two models are not definitionally the same.  At every natural order ,\nthe real physical subspace of  is continuously real-linearly equivalent\nto the integer graph space .  The equivalence has\nexplicit bounds in both directions. The norms differ, because Mathlib's\nFourier transform has derivative multiplier\n while PDEAtlas uses the unscaled Bessel symbol .\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Sobolev Coverage and Open Problems\u0009The two Sobolev models","header":"4.6.1.1.2. Fourier Bessel-potential spaces","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Sobolev-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--The-two-Sobolev-models--Fourier-Bessel-potential-spaces"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Earlier-approaches":{"contents":"There are two established ways to obtain this guarantee. An external algebra\nsystem can propose an answer that a proof assistant checks. Lewis and Wu's\nLean–Mathematica interface follows this approach: it translates expressions\nbetween the systems and reconstructs proofs for selected returned computations\n. The search can be powerful without making\nthe external calculator part of the proof's trusted basis.\n\nAlternatively, an algorithm can be defined inside the proof assistant and\nproved correct once. Computational reflection evaluates that algorithm on an\nexpression tree and applies its correctness theorem; Boutin developed this\napproach for certified decision procedures in Coq\n. PDEAtlas uses this pattern for its supported\nsymbolic transformations and interval evaluators. The\nRelated Work chapter compares the surrounding projects.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation","header":"10.1. Earlier approaches","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Earlier-approaches"}});